 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed and do not depend on how lookups are resolved. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookups resolve against a local embedded SQLite index that each kiosk builds from the active snapshot; no service or network path is involved. The backend is private to this node — its presence does not reach the certificates interface.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. From a snapshot that passes validation, build a fresh SQLite index in a staging location, keyed by the normalized serial number, without exposing partially imported records. Activate the new index only after the build and all validation succeed. An import failure — bad signature, unsupported schema, duplicate serials, or a failed index build — leaves the previously active index in place and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active SQLite index for the normalized serial on its indexed key. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt on each kiosk when a snapshot is imported | On-site HTTPS directory (one central index, smaller kiosk updates); managed search service (less index maintenance, room for richer search later) | Keeps all lookup on files present on the kiosk, satisfying the no-runtime-network tenets; no service to run and predictable lookup latency. The networked alternatives would make lookups depend on the lab network, credentials, and external connectivity — outside those tenets — in exchange for central index maintenance the kiosk would otherwise do itself. |
